A||||9||PAUL88GT||Heater/AC fan not working correctly||||1||
Z||000000||PAUL88GT||07-14-2000||01:57 PM||||My heater/AC fan does not come on immediately when the switch in one of the on positions, but the fan comes on  and works properly after a few minutes. It like it has some kind of a delay built in.  Is this problem with the switch or could it be the resistor that I have heard mentioned before? TIA.<BR>Paul ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Taijiguy||07-14-2000||02:07 PM||||Some of the systems use an A/C high pressure switch, that only activates the fan when the high side of the A/C reaches about 280 psi. If the fan cycles omore or less along with the compressor, then most likely your system is working properly. <BR>There will be a slight delay for the fan to come on, as the pressure switch waits for the system pressure to rise, and for it to go off, as it waits for the system pressure to drop.<P>Taiji<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 07-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||86se/lambo||07-16-2000||01:11 AM||||i believe taijaguy that the high pressure switch is to not allow the compressor to engage at extreme a/c pressures. should not have any effect on the operation of the fan switch... maybe i mis understood the question.||||reg||1
Z||000003||86se/lambo||07-16-2000||01:14 AM||||what fan is not coming on the one at the radiator or the blower fan inside ?||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||07-16-2000||12:32 PM||||AC pressure switches only control compressor activation.<P>Both the heater blower and radiator fan should come on when the system is put in any AC mode. you heater blower by itself only in one or 2 modes. they both should come on instantly when activated.<P>I'd suspect the heater motor. check to see if it's getting power. It sound like it may be getting power but has some sort of internal problem. <P>It could be a resistor but that usually only effects one speed. if it's affecting all speeds then you should be able to see why by taking the block out and looking at it. Even if it is a resistor it should always work on high since that is straight wired. If it wpn't work on high it's either a motor or control problem.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||07-16-2000||08:45 PM||||Actually, after rereading the post, I think I mis-understood the question. However, the A/C system has three pressure switches, the first being a low pressure switch, which is in line with the compressor relay, to prevent the compressor from coming on when there's no charge in the system. And another which is a high pressure switch, which opens at something around 420 psi to kill the compressor. The other (which isn't always in the system) is another high pressure switch, and it closes when the a/c high side reaches 280 psi. If you're familiar with air conditioning, you know that high side pressure can be upwards of 300 psi in operation, so to have a switch that cuts the compressor at 280 psi would be counter productive. The second high side switch is there to activate the condensing unit fan (or radiator fan, as it is) so that the fan is only running when the a/c is actually operating, and not just "on". There are probably as many systems that don't use the high side switch, and just have the fan on whenever the a/c is turned on, whether it's actually running or not.<BR>As for the true nature of the problem, if it's the blower motor that doesn't come on till after a delay, that could be anyone's guess.<P>-Taiji<P><p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 07-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||07-17-2000||01:11 PM||||Taiji,<P>Fiero's system doesn't use the high switches to control blowers as some do. The blower & rad fan are on whenever an AC control is on. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||286SE'S||07-17-2000||01:44 PM||||The fan is easy to pull in the front tire well. Take it out and see if it spins freely by hand. If not it may need lubrication. It sounds to me like it is just taking its time fighting the hardened grease and once it gets going the heat from the friction allows full spin. I have seen this on a lot of cheap 20" box fans from K-mart.<BR>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000008||PAUL88GT||07-17-2000||02:35 PM||||It is the fan inside the car, not the radiator fan. Thanks for all the responses. I will pull the fan and and see if it is sticking. I know it is getting power sometimes, because it comes a few minutes after driving the car.   ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Taijiguy||07-17-2000||09:52 PM||||Ogre,<P>Although I haven't physically checked, the prints in the Chilton's manual show a few different configurations which use the fan conrol switch. (That's where the 280 psi figure came from) Not all systems use it, but some do.<P>Later-<P>Taiji||||reg||1
